#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092020 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092360 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0e3b20b5-9427-538d-9392-aa137b5d8126" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the First Term Exceeding a Given Value"}] :question/id #uuid "c3aac9bd-c462-5664-a196-278a9671fbbb" :question/math-academy-id "e-4243" :question/problem "What is the first term that exceeds $2\\,000\\,000$ in the following geometric sequence?\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 1 {32},\\: \\dfrac 1 4,\\: 2,\\: 16,\\: \\ldots\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The first term of the sequence is $a_1=\\dfrac{1}{32}$ and the common ratio is\n\n$$\nr = \\dfrac {\\left(\\dfrac 1 {4}\\right)} {\\left(\\dfrac 1 {32} \\right)} = 8.\n$$\n\nSo, the $n$th term of the sequence can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{32}\\cdot {8}^{n-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{64}\\cdot {8}^{n-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{8}^{2}}\\cdot {8}^{n-1} \\\\  & =2\\cdot {8}^{-2}\\cdot {8}^{n-1} \\\\  & =2\\cdot {8}^{n-3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want the $n$th term to be greater than $2\\,000\\,000.$ So, we set up and solve the corresponding inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\cdot {8}^{n-3} & >2\\,000\\,000 \\\\ {8}^{n-3} & >1\\,000\\,000 \\\\ \\operatorname{log}⁡({8}^{n-3}) & >\\operatorname{log}⁡(1\\,000\\,000) \\\\ (n-3)\\operatorname{log}⁡(8) & >\\operatorname{log}⁡(1\\,000\\,000) \\\\ n-3 & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(1\\,000\\,000)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(8)} \\\\ n & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(1\\,000\\,000)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(8)}+3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEstimating the right-hand side using a calculator, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\log (1\\,000\\, 000)} {\\log (8)} + 3 \\approx 9.643.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $10$th term is the first term of the sequence that exceeds $2\\,000\\,000.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092021 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092361 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "43dcbb7e-ba21-506f-a706-bfb28371e0a9" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the First Term Smaller Than a Given Value"}] :question/id #uuid "4af09b88-5f57-572f-ae64-bfe64e94450d" :question/math-academy-id "e-4244" :question/problem "What is the first term that is less than $0. 000\\,000\\,1$ in the following geometric sequence?\n\n$$\n16,\\: 8,\\: 4,\\: 2,\\: \\ldots\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The first term of the sequence is $a_1=16,$ and the common ratio is\n\n$$\nr = \\dfrac 8 {16}  = \\dfrac 1 2.\n$$\n\nSo, the $n$th term of the sequence can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & =16\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{2})}^{n-1} \\\\  & ={2}^{4}\\cdot {2}^{1-n} \\\\  & ={2}^{5-n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want the $n$th term to be less than $0.000\\,000\\,1.$ So, we set up and solve the corresponding inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2}^{5-n} & <0.000\\,000\\,1 \\\\ \\operatorname{log}⁡({2}^{5-n}) & <\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,000\\,1) \\\\ (5-n)\\operatorname{log}⁡(2) & <\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,000\\,1) \\\\ 5-n & <\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,000\\,1)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(2)} \\\\ -n & <\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,000\\,1)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(2)}-5 \\\\ n & >5-\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,000\\,1)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(2)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEstimating the right-hand side using a calculator, we get\n\n$$\n5 - \\dfrac { \\log (0.000\\, 000\\, 1)} {\\log (2)} \\approx 28.253.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $29$th term is the first term of the sequence that is less than $0. 000\\,000\\,1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092022 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092362 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8c8f1095-0b60-5dad-8471-4efa453e9d4b" :knowledge-point/title "Cases Where the First Term Is Unknown"}] :question/id #uuid "154a5006-6c4f-58c7-a228-ccbefabd5050" :question/math-academy-id "e-4245" :question/problem "The $4$th term of a geometric sequence is $54,$ and the common ratio is $3.$ What is the first term of this sequence to exceed\n$1\\,000?$" :question/worked-solution "Since we are given that $a_{4}= 54$ and  $r= 3,$ we can use the $n$th term formula to solve for $a_1,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{a_n&=a_1\\cdot r^{n-1}\\[3pt]\n\t\ta_4&=a_1\\cdot 3^{4-1}\\[3pt]\n\t54&=a_1\\cdot 3^3\\[3pt]\n\t54&=27a_1\\[3pt]\n\ta_1&=2\n\t}\n$$\n\nSo, the $n$th term of the geometric sequence is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & =2\\cdot {3}^{n-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want the $n$th term to be greater than $1\\,000.$ So, we set up and solve the corresponding inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\cdot {3}^{n-1} & >1\\,000 \\\\ {3}^{n-1} & >500 \\\\ \\operatorname{log}⁡(3)^{n-1} & >\\operatorname{log}⁡(500) \\\\ (n-1)\\operatorname{log}⁡(3) & >\\operatorname{log}⁡(500) \\\\ n-1 & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(500)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(3)} \\\\ n & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(500)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(3)}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEstimating the right-hand side using a calculator, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\log (500)} {\\log (3)} + 1 \\approx 6.657.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $7$th term is the first term of the sequence that exceeds $1\\,000.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186573942 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092360 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0e3b20b5-9427-538d-9392-aa137b5d8126" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the First Term Exceeding a Given Value"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "472ee2cf-f213-551c-a5c8-f50b35a5f1b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the $14$th term" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186573510} {:answer/id #uuid "aa87cd4c-68b9-5473-b187-90c3182c3907" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the $16$th term" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186573647} {:answer/id #uuid "b045390f-73da-5355-846a-f0ce0908613c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the $12$th term" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186573653} {:answer/id #uuid "bdc3f14a-bd8f-51ec-bbf4-18e3aa041510" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the $21$th term" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186573670} {:answer/id #uuid "c8a44c17-cc2f-581a-8267-f012c362034b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the $18$th term" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186573686}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "aa87cd4c-68b9-5473-b187-90c3182c3907" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the $16$th term" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186573647} :answer-field/id #uuid "62cdce21-f8a1-5862-a134-d10fa57837ce" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186573813}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "f1da87a1-4d7e-5076-b588-28216bd9d666" :question/math-academy-id "q-218497" :question/problem "What is the first term that exceeds $1\\,200\\,000$ in the following geometric sequence?\n\n$$\n3,\\,3\\sqrt{6},\\,18,\\,18\\sqrt{6},\\,…\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The first term of the sequence is ${a}_{1}=3$ and the common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{3\\sqrt{6}}{3}=\\sqrt{6}.\n$$\n\nSo, the $n$th term of the sequence can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & =3\\cdot (\\sqrt{6})^{n-1} \\\\  & =3\\cdot {6}^{(n-1)/2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want the $n$th term to be greater than $1\\,200\\,000.$\nHence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & >1\\,200\\,000 \\\\ 3\\cdot {6}^{(n-1)/2} & >1\\,200\\,000 \\\\ {6}^{(n-1)/2} & >400\\,000 \\\\ \\operatorname{log}⁡({6}^{(n-1)/2}) & >\\operatorname{log}⁡(400\\,000) \\\\ \\frac{(n-1)}{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{log}⁡(6) & >\\operatorname{log}⁡(400\\,000) \\\\ \\frac{(n-1)}{2} & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(400\\,000)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(6)} \\\\ n-1 & >\\frac{2\\operatorname{log}⁡(400\\,000)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(6)} \\\\ n & >\\frac{2\\operatorname{log}⁡(400\\,000)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(6)}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEstimating the right-hand side using a calculator, we get:\n\n$$\n\\frac{2\\operatorname{log}⁡(400\\,000)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(6)}+1≈15.398\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $16$th term is the first term of the sequence that exceeds $1\\,200\\,000.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186573907 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092360 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0e3b20b5-9427-538d-9392-aa137b5d8126" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the First Term Exceeding a Given Value"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7f43b9ff-c57d-5176-8447-e1c240ac0c06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "19" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186573590}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7f43b9ff-c57d-5176-8447-e1c240ac0c06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "19" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186573590} :answer-field/id #uuid "82f102b3-cfde-50a7-befa-6d1aba4b3795" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186573823}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a4a0b4ff-96f6-5f22-9a8c-2470e817fb96" :question/math-academy-id "q-270985" :question/problem "What is the first term that exceeds  $2\\,000\\,000$ in the following geometric sequence?\n\n$$\n\\,\\,\\,\\,2,\\,2\\sqrt{5},\\,10,\\,10\\sqrt{5},\\,…\n$$\n\nThe first term that exceeds $2\\,000\\,000$ is the\n{{field-1}}th term." :question/worked-solution "The first term of the sequence is ${a}_{1}=2$ and the common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{2}=\\sqrt{5}.\n$$\n\nSo, the $n$th term of the sequence can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & =2\\cdot {(\\sqrt{5})}^{n-1} \\\\  & =2\\cdot {5}^{(n-1)/2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want the $n$th term to be greater than $2\\,000\\,000.$\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & >2\\,000\\,000 \\\\ 2\\cdot {5}^{(n-1)/2} & >2\\,000\\,000 \\\\ {5}^{(n-1)/2} & >1\\,000\\,000 \\\\ \\operatorname{log}⁡({5}^{(n-1)/2}) & >\\operatorname{log}⁡(1\\,000\\,000) \\\\ \\frac{(n-1)}{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{log}⁡(5) & >\\operatorname{log}⁡(1\\,000\\,000) \\\\ \\frac{(n-1)}{2} & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(1\\,000\\,000)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(5)} \\\\ n-1 & >\\frac{2\\operatorname{log}⁡(1\\,000\\,000)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(5)} \\\\ n & >\\frac{2\\operatorname{log}⁡(1\\,000\\,000)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(5)}+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEstimating the right-hand side using a calculator, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{2\\operatorname{log}⁡(1\\,000\\,000)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(5)}+1≈18.168.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $19$th term is the first term of the sequence that exceeds $2\\,000\\,000.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186573940 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092361 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "43dcbb7e-ba21-506f-a706-bfb28371e0a9" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the First Term Smaller Than a Given Value"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e50b1bba-38d1-5105-8700-91d45010831f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186573726}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e50b1bba-38d1-5105-8700-91d45010831f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186573726} :answer-field/id #uuid "169c3464-e1d8-5414-be39-fcd17c8d800f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186573779}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ebe8e960-0d95-59ae-8282-f9852d97bd86" :question/math-academy-id "q-270990" :question/problem "What is the first term that is less than $0.000\\,000\\,5$ in the following geometric sequence?\n\n$$\n\\,\\,\\,\\,256,\\,64,\\,16,\\,4,\\,…\n$$\n\nThe first term that is less than $0.000\\,000\\,5$ is the\n{{field-1}}th term." :question/worked-solution "The first term of the sequence is ${a}_{1}=256$ and the common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{64}{256}=\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\nSo, the $n$th term of the sequence can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & =256\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{4})}^{n-1} \\\\  & ={4}^{4}\\cdot {4}^{1-n} \\\\  & ={4}^{5-n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want the $n$th term to be less than $0.000\\,000\\,5.$\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & <0.000\\,000\\,5 \\\\ {4}^{5-n} & <0.000\\,000\\,5 \\\\ \\operatorname{log}⁡({4}^{5-n}) & <\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,000\\,5) \\\\ (5-n)\\operatorname{log}⁡(4) & <\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,000\\,5) \\\\ 5-n & <\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,000\\,5)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(4)} \\\\ -n & <\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,000\\,5)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(4)}-5 \\\\ n & >5-\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,000\\,5)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(4)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEstimating the right-hand side using a calculator, we get\n\n$$\n5-\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,000\\,5)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(4)}≈15.466.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $16$th term is the first term of the sequence that is less than $0.000\\,000\\,5.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294937 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092360 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0e3b20b5-9427-538d-9392-aa137b5d8126" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the First Term Exceeding a Given Value"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dab19498-0f30-5fbf-b741-364f1b7f05f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293779}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dab19498-0f30-5fbf-b741-364f1b7f05f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293779} :answer-field/id #uuid "56b7f67d-5586-52c4-a88d-588629b42516" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294237}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "29e6ffa8-9f33-56f5-810e-d7c6edc1eff6" :question/math-academy-id "q-277931" :question/problem "What is the first term that exceeds $58\\,000$ in the following geometric sequence?\n\n$$\n2,\\,8,\\,32,\\,128,\\,…\n$$\n\nThe first term that exceeds $58\\,000$ is the\n{{field-1}}th term." :question/worked-solution "The first term of the sequence is ${a}_{1}=2$ and the common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{8}{2}=4.\n$$\n\nSo, the $n$th term of the sequence can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & =2\\cdot {4}^{n-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want the $n$th term to be greater than $58\\,000.$\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & >58\\,000 \\\\ 2\\cdot {4}^{n-1} & >58\\,000 \\\\ {4}^{n-1} & >29\\,000 \\\\ \\operatorname{log}⁡({4}^{n-1}) & >\\operatorname{log}⁡(29\\,000) \\\\ (n-1)\\operatorname{log}⁡(4) & >\\operatorname{log}⁡(29\\,000) \\\\ n-1 & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(29\\,000)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(4)} \\\\ n & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(29\\,000)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(4)}+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEstimating the right-hand side using a calculator, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(29\\,000)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(4)}+1≈8.412.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $9$th term is the first term of the sequence that exceeds $58\\,000.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186573912 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092360 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0e3b20b5-9427-538d-9392-aa137b5d8126" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the First Term Exceeding a Given Value"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ac01b05-3fb7-5830-b47a-99dc18dd32a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186573437}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0ac01b05-3fb7-5830-b47a-99dc18dd32a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186573437} :answer-field/id #uuid "7d575c13-2f02-5429-a72c-36a467924c30" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186573820}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ac65bd0d-2582-576c-9ae6-671d41f53032" :question/math-academy-id "q-277938" :question/problem "What is the first term that exceeds  $1\\,000$ in the following geometric sequence?\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{2},\\,2,\\,2\\sqrt{2},\\,4,\\,…\n$$\n\nThe first term that exceeds $1\\,000$ is the\n{{field-1}}th term." :question/worked-solution "The first term of the sequence is ${a}_{1}=\\sqrt{2}$ and the common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{2}}=\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nSo, the $n$th term of the sequence can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\cdot {(\\sqrt{2})}^{n-1} \\\\  & ={2}^{n/2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want the $n$th term to be greater than $1\\,000.$\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & >1\\,000 \\\\ {2}^{n/2} & >1\\,000 \\\\ \\operatorname{log}⁡({2}^{n/2}) & >\\operatorname{log}⁡(1\\,000) \\\\ \\frac{n}{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{log}⁡(2) & >\\operatorname{log}⁡(1\\,000) \\\\ \\frac{n}{2} & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(1\\,000)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(2)} \\\\ n & >\\frac{2\\operatorname{log}⁡(1\\,000)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(2)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEstimating the right-hand side using a calculator, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{2\\operatorname{log}⁡(1\\,000)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(2)}≈19.932.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $20$th term is the first term of the sequence that exceeds $1\\,000.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186573887 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092361 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "43dcbb7e-ba21-506f-a706-bfb28371e0a9" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the First Term Smaller Than a Given Value"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a0a558a6-da45-59d3-bc0d-39cedb9594ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186573634}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a0a558a6-da45-59d3-bc0d-39cedb9594ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186573634} :answer-field/id #uuid "8cac1aa5-f45b-54bd-a714-87c9a836435c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186573825}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "59bb5b60-160e-59f4-91ef-a981e2cb8f1e" :question/math-academy-id "q-278075" :question/problem "What is the first term that is less than $0.000\\,4$ in the following geometric sequence?\n\n$$\n128,\\,16,\\,2,\\,\\frac{1}{4},\\,…\n$$\n\nThe first term that is less than $0.000\\,4$ is the\n{{field-1}}th term." :question/worked-solution "The first term of the sequence is ${a}_{1}=128$ and the common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{16}{128}=\\frac{1}{8}.\n$$\n\nSo, the $n$th term of the sequence can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & =128{(\\frac{1}{8})}^{n-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want the $n$th term to be less than $0.000\\,4.$\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & <0.000\\,4 \\\\ 128{(\\frac{1}{8})}^{n-1} & <0.000\\,4 \\\\ {(\\frac{1}{8})}^{n-1} & <\\frac{0.000\\,4}{128} \\\\ \\operatorname{log}⁡{(\\frac{1}{8})}^{n-1} & <\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,003\\,125) \\\\ (n-1)\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{1}{8}) & <\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,003\\,125) \\\\ n-1 & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,003\\,125)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{1}{8})} \\\\ n & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,003\\,125)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{1}{8})}+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we reversed the inequality sign when divided by $\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{1}{8})$ because $\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{1}{8})$ is a negative number. \n\nEstimating the right-hand side using a calculator, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,003\\,125)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{1}{8})}+1≈7.096.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $8$th term is the first term of the sequence that is less than $0.000\\,4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186573906 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092361 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "43dcbb7e-ba21-506f-a706-bfb28371e0a9" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the First Term Smaller Than a Given Value"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "55d9002e-7527-50cc-b173-50109757113f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186573530}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "55d9002e-7527-50cc-b173-50109757113f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186573530} :answer-field/id #uuid "41e36901-ce9f-5fd2-ac48-592419a20007" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186573798}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8cb20152-3f82-5426-99bf-afc35d2d2b76" :question/math-academy-id "q-278076" :question/problem "What is the first term that is less than $1$ in the following geometric sequence?\n\n$$\n125,\\,75,\\,45,\\,27,\\,…\n$$\n\nThe first term that is less than $1$ is the\n{{field-1}}th term." :question/worked-solution "The first term of the sequence is ${a}_{1}=125$ and the common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{75}{125}=\\frac{3}{5}.\n$$\n\nSo, the $n$th term of the sequence can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & =125{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{n-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want the $n$th term to be less than $1.$\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & <1 \\\\ 125{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{n-1} & <1 \\\\ {(\\frac{3}{5})}^{n-1} & <\\frac{1}{125} \\\\ \\operatorname{log}⁡{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{n-1} & <\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.008) \\\\ (n-1)\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{3}{5}) & <\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.008) \\\\ n-1 & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.008)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{3}{5})} \\\\ n & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.008)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{3}{5})}+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we reversed the inequality sign when divided by $\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{3}{5})$ because $\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{3}{5})$ is a negative number. \n\nEstimating the right-hand side using a calculator, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.008)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{3}{5})}+1≈10.452.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $11$th term is the first term of the sequence that is less than $1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186573935 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092362 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8c8f1095-0b60-5dad-8471-4efa453e9d4b" :knowledge-point/title "Cases Where the First Term Is Unknown"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5117e655-c6d3-59fa-a58b-c792581abb27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186573521}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5117e655-c6d3-59fa-a58b-c792581abb27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186573521} :answer-field/id #uuid "4d601646-0a54-5c73-8308-0c6b5ed39be8" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186573801}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e0ccb719-0526-539f-8346-82d0473506ac" :question/math-academy-id "q-278084" :question/problem "The $4$th term of a geometric sequence is $192$, and the common ratio is $\\frac{4}{3}.$ The first term of this sequence to exceed $3\\,078$ is the{{field-1}} th term." :question/worked-solution "Since we are given that ${a}_{4}=192$ and  $r=\\frac{4}{3}$, we can use the $n$th term formula to solve for ${a}_{1}$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\ {a}_{4} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {(\\frac{4}{3})}^{4-1} \\\\ 192 & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {(\\frac{4}{3})}^{3} \\\\ 192 & =\\frac{64}{27}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{1} & =81\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the $n$th term of the geometric sequence is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & =81\\cdot {(\\frac{4}{3})}^{n-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want $n$th term to be greater than $3\\,078.$\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}81\\cdot {(\\frac{4}{3})}^{n-1} & >3\\,078 \\\\ {(\\frac{4}{3})}^{n-1} & >38 \\\\ \\operatorname{log}⁡{(\\frac{4}{3})}^{n-1} & >\\operatorname{log}⁡(38) \\\\ (n-1)\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{4}{3}) & >\\operatorname{log}⁡(38) \\\\ n-1 & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(38)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{4}{3})} \\\\ n & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(38)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{4}{3})}+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEstimating the right-hand side using a calculator, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(38)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{4}{3})}+1≈13.644.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $14$th term is the first term of the sequence that exceeds $3\\,078.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186573885 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092362 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8c8f1095-0b60-5dad-8471-4efa453e9d4b" :knowledge-point/title "Cases Where the First Term Is Unknown"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08c2b08f-4b86-5a28-b207-246ddaeede47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186573433}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "08c2b08f-4b86-5a28-b207-246ddaeede47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186573433} :answer-field/id #uuid "fa757127-e918-5b58-b712-d77618d9d911" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186573862}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "525be19b-f128-5226-ba8b-06889781710d" :question/math-academy-id "q-278085" :question/problem "The $3$rd term of a geometric sequence is $200$, and the common ratio is $\\frac{5}{4}.$ The first term of this sequence to exceed $8\\,000$ is the{{field-1}} th term." :question/worked-solution "Since we are given that ${a}_{3}=200$ and  $r=\\frac{5}{4}$, we can use the $n$th term formula to solve for ${a}_{1}$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\ {a}_{3} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {(\\frac{5}{4})}^{3-1} \\\\ 200 & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {(\\frac{5}{4})}^{2} \\\\ 200 & =\\frac{25}{16}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{1} & =128\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the $n$th term of the geometric sequence is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & =128\\cdot {(\\frac{5}{4})}^{n-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want $n$th term to be greater than $8\\,000.$\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}128\\cdot {(\\frac{4}{3})}^{n-1} & >8\\,000 \\\\ {(\\frac{5}{4})}^{n-1} & >62.5 \\\\ \\operatorname{log}⁡{(\\frac{5}{4})}^{n-1} & >\\operatorname{log}⁡(62.5) \\\\ (n-1)\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{5}{4}) & >\\operatorname{log}⁡(62.5) \\\\ n-1 & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(62.5)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{5}{4})} \\\\ n & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(62.5)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{5}{4})}+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEstimating the right-hand side using a calculator, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(62.5)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{5}{4})}+1≈19.531.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $20$th term is the first term of the sequence that exceeds $8\\,000.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186573875 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092362 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8c8f1095-0b60-5dad-8471-4efa453e9d4b" :knowledge-point/title "Cases Where the First Term Is Unknown"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "728351c9-2bf6-53fe-a20e-05a5e81b391d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186573567}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "728351c9-2bf6-53fe-a20e-05a5e81b391d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186573567} :answer-field/id #uuid "4bb175f0-c06d-5380-9553-6a725410719e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186573800}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "1d00b5b7-e529-5ab1-8ece-1078cbcb48a1" :question/math-academy-id "q-278086" :question/problem "The $3$rd term of a geometric sequence is $18$, and the $6$th term is $486.$ The first term of this sequence to exceed $10\\,000$ is the{{field-1}} th term." :question/worked-solution "Since we are given that ${a}_{3}=18$ and ${a}_{6}=486$, we can find the common ratio $r$ of the sequence, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{n-m} & =\\frac{{a}_{n}}{{a}_{m}} \\\\ {r}^{6-3} & =\\frac{{a}_{6}}{{a}_{3}} \\\\ {r}^{3} & =\\frac{486}{18} \\\\ {r}^{3} & =27 \\\\ r & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since ${a}_{3}=18$ and  $r=3$, we can use the $n$th term formula to solve for ${a}_{1}$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\ {a}_{3} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {3}^{3-1} \\\\ 18 & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {3}^{2} \\\\ 18 & =9{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{1} & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the $n$th term of the geometric sequence is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & =2\\cdot {3}^{n-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want $n$th term to be greater than $10\\,000.$\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\cdot {3}^{n-1} & >10\\,000 \\\\ {3}^{n-1} & >5\\,000 \\\\ \\operatorname{log}⁡({3}^{n-1}) & >\\operatorname{log}⁡(5\\,000) \\\\ (n-1)\\operatorname{log}⁡(3) & >\\operatorname{log}⁡(5\\,000) \\\\ n-1 & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(5\\,000)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(3)} \\\\ n & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(5\\,000)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(3)}+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEstimating the right-hand side using a calculator, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(5\\,000)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(3)}+1≈8.753.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $9$th term is the first term of the sequence that exceeds $10\\,000.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326772 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092361 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "43dcbb7e-ba21-506f-a706-bfb28371e0a9" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the First Term Smaller Than a Given Value"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "24eebd01-d696-5583-866b-bc7ee0975ae6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the $42$th term" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324275} {:answer/id #uuid "5efa3e05-6fbd-561f-aad6-9039ff65a874" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the $39$th term" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324765} {:answer/id #uuid "869369c2-c60a-5bfe-9e90-d7fc384dd4c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the $38$th term" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325115} {:answer/id #uuid "e425578a-3944-5da8-bbf6-a67efada93ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the $41$th term" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325979} {:answer/id #uuid "e7c67aa3-aae6-50c4-9e30-cdb98ed82129" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the $40$th term" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326012}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e7c67aa3-aae6-50c4-9e30-cdb98ed82129" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the $40$th term" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326012} :answer-field/id #uuid "1793d83a-a4d4-5328-b990-9f3e282f524a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326264}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b5d1d92f-ad9b-5aee-bc72-594097ea04d9" :question/math-academy-id "q-39648" :question/problem "What is the first term that is less than $0.001$ in the following geometric sequence?\n\n$$\n64,\\,48,\\,36,\\,27,\\,…\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The first term of the sequence is ${a}_{1}=64$ and the common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{48}{64}=\\frac{3}{4}.\n$$\n\nSo, the $n$th term of the sequence can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & =64{(\\frac{3}{4})}^{n-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want the $n$th term to be less than $0.001.$\nHence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & <0.001 \\\\ 64{(\\frac{3}{4})}^{n-1} & <0.001 \\\\ {(\\frac{3}{4})}^{n-1} & <\\frac{0.001}{64} \\\\ \\operatorname{log}⁡{(\\frac{3}{4})}^{n-1} & <\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,016) \\\\ (n-1)\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{3}{4}) & <\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,016) \\\\ n-1 & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,016)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{3}{4})} \\\\ n & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,016)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{3}{4})}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we reversed the inequality sign when divided by $\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{3}{4})$ because $\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{3}{4})$ is a negative number. \n\nEstimating the right-hand side using a calculator, we get:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,016)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{3}{4})}+1≈39.386\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $40$th term is the first term of the sequence that is less than $0.001.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186573915 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092361 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "43dcbb7e-ba21-506f-a706-bfb28371e0a9" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the First Term Smaller Than a Given Value"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1939f72d-64d6-54bc-bd4a-8b8702fde750" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the $17$th term" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186573448} {:answer/id #uuid "6e861090-f70a-554c-874f-5299c3170e4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the $16$th term" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186573559} {:answer/id #uuid "c758b9b0-3e71-524d-b24e-646ee983b547" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the $14$th term" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186573681} {:answer/id #uuid "cccbc5f5-87bb-59f1-bad5-8c3a748710a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the $15$th term" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186573693} {:answer/id #uuid "ed3d62c8-3dd0-5c05-859f-f145a44be51a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the $18$th term" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186573737}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6e861090-f70a-554c-874f-5299c3170e4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the $16$th term" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186573559} :answer-field/id #uuid "f1ab172e-649a-555e-92e9-4be54273ab03" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186573855}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b67cf463-2810-5439-8050-fefe332f47c4" :question/math-academy-id "q-39650" :question/problem "What is the first term that is less than $0.000\\,3$ in the following geometric sequence?\n\n$$\n125,\\,50,\\,20,\\,8,\\,…\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The first term of the sequence is ${a}_{1}=125$ and the common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{50}{125}=\\frac{2}{5}.\n$$\n\nSo, the $n$th term of the sequence can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & =125{(\\frac{2}{5})}^{n-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want the $n$th term to be less than $0.000\\,3.$\nHence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & <0.000\\,3 \\\\ 125{(\\frac{2}{5})}^{n-1} & <0.000\\,3 \\\\ {(\\frac{2}{5})}^{n-1} & <\\frac{0.000\\,3}{125} \\\\ \\operatorname{log}⁡{(\\frac{2}{5})}^{n-1} & <\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,002\\,4) \\\\ (n-1)\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{2}{5}) & <\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,002\\,4) \\\\ n-1 & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,002\\,4)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{2}{5})} \\\\ n & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,002\\,4)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{2}{5})}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we reversed the inequality sign when divided by $\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{2}{5})$ because $\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{2}{5})$ is a negative number. \n\nEstimating the right-hand side using a calculator, we get:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,002\\,4)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{2}{5})}+1≈15.122\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $16$th term is the first term of the sequence that is less than $0.000\\,3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186573936 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092362 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8c8f1095-0b60-5dad-8471-4efa453e9d4b" :knowledge-point/title "Cases Where the First Term Is Unknown"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0edb8693-647d-59b4-b760-4e1b199e6824" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the $16$th term" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186573440} {:answer/id #uuid "209399c3-a38a-5613-9a3a-1c6de859cd72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the $13$th term" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186573459} {:answer/id #uuid "5b5413ab-bc06-5845-9a9d-5f8befa9750a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the $15$th term" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186573542} {:answer/id #uuid "a0d3486e-bf73-59ce-9808-d924ea025065" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the $11$th term" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186573635} {:answer/id #uuid "f7d1d824-5393-515b-b997-5c61c2e7069c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the $14$th term" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186573760}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f7d1d824-5393-515b-b997-5c61c2e7069c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the $14$th term" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186573760} :answer-field/id #uuid "67ba2bb6-0524-5fd3-9617-ca3e6c50fb7d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186573816}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e0f0f519-39b1-5bf5-a763-c3c4f5d95f00" :question/math-academy-id "q-39653" :question/problem "The $6$th term of a geometric sequence is $\\frac{1}{4}$, and the common ratio is $\\frac{1}{2}.$ What is the first term of this sequence to be less than\n$0.001\\,2?$" :question/worked-solution "Since we are given that ${a}_{6}=\\frac{1}{4}$ and  $r=\\frac{1}{2}$, we can use the $n$th term formula to solve for ${a}_{1}$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\ {a}_{6} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{2})}^{6-1} \\\\ \\frac{1}{4} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{2}^{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{4} & =\\frac{1}{32}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{1} & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the $n$th term of the geometric sequence is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & =8\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{2})}^{n-1} \\\\  & ={2}^{3}\\cdot {2}^{1-n} \\\\  & ={2}^{4-n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want the $n$th term to be less than $0.001\\,2.$\nHence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2}^{4-n} & <0.001\\,2 \\\\ \\operatorname{log}⁡(2)^{4-n} & <\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.001\\,2) \\\\ (4-n)\\operatorname{log}⁡(2) & <\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.001\\,2) \\\\ 4-n & <\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.001\\,2)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(2)} \\\\ -n & <\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.001\\,2)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(2)}-4 \\\\ n & >4-\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.001\\,2)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(2)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEstimating the right-hand side using a calculator, we get:\n\n$$\n4-\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.001\\,2)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(2)}≈13.703\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $14$th term is the first term of the sequence that is less than $0.001\\,2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335932 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092362 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8c8f1095-0b60-5dad-8471-4efa453e9d4b" :knowledge-point/title "Cases Where the First Term Is Unknown"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "31f79c15-e015-58ee-9759-1651faf25e40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the $10$th term" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333447} {:answer/id #uuid "398d4c7a-0785-51dd-a6f3-ad5515ef3183" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the $12$th term" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333527} {:answer/id #uuid "45ca5716-9eb2-586f-a070-fc824e9589a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the $13$th term" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333633} {:answer/id #uuid "56d76ff2-b484-54dd-a935-e81f1b63a229" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the $11$th term" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333775} {:answer/id #uuid "e8e570b2-ef64-5bdf-9d62-b47beca6903d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the $14$th term" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335022}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "398d4c7a-0785-51dd-a6f3-ad5515ef3183" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the $12$th term" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333527} :answer-field/id #uuid "e85b7624-09f8-51bb-bb59-2a37f079db87" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335644}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f5456760-9cdb-54e3-82ce-b6d6e704f8ce" :question/math-academy-id "q-67516" :question/problem "The $6$th term of a geometric sequence is $3$, and the common ratio is $3.$ What is the first term of this sequence to exceed\n$907?$" :question/worked-solution "Since we are given that ${a}_{6}=3$ and  $r=3$, we can use the $n$th term formula to solve for ${a}_{1}$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\ {a}_{6} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {3}^{6-1} \\\\ 3 & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {3}^{5} \\\\ \\frac{1}{{3}^{4}} & ={a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{81}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the $n$th term of the geometric sequence is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{81}\\cdot {3}^{n-1} \\\\  & ={3}^{-4}\\cdot {3}^{n-1} \\\\  & ={3}^{n-5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want $n$th term to be greater than $907.$\nHence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{n-5} & >907 \\\\ \\operatorname{log}⁡{(3)}^{n-5} & >\\operatorname{log}⁡(907) \\\\ (n-5)\\operatorname{log}⁡(3) & >\\operatorname{log}⁡(907) \\\\ n-5 & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(907)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(3)} \\\\ n & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(907)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(3)}+5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEstimating the right-hand side using a calculator, we get:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(907)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(3)}+5≈11.199\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $12$th term is the first term of the sequence that exceeds $907.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360806 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092360 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0e3b20b5-9427-538d-9392-aa137b5d8126" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the First Term Exceeding a Given Value"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a68a45c-7e16-5fb2-afee-3dfbb5fdb55b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the $9$th term" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357841} {:answer/id #uuid "42a3c94b-95c3-5072-8e13-4256db80a58f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the $7$th term" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358320} {:answer/id #uuid "a1a780c1-259a-5ea0-bd30-a23c589220b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the $10$th term" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359160} {:answer/id #uuid "caa963c4-4072-5bbe-b660-3ffaf8cff6bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the $6$th term" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359530} {:answer/id #uuid "f588cff2-7042-54f7-9627-96d8e9d8f0ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the $8$th term" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359928}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f588cff2-7042-54f7-9627-96d8e9d8f0ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the $8$th term" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359928} :answer-field/id #uuid "e90aa030-babb-5358-a2e1-4ec64e1f8392" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360408}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "95917747-a74b-509e-9924-fa8c1433f09e" :question/math-academy-id "q-9743" :question/problem "What is the first term that exceeds $2\\,000\\,000$ in the following geometric sequence?\n\n$$\n4,\\,28,\\,196,\\,1\\,372,\\,…\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The first term of the sequence is ${a}_{1}=4$ and the common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{28}{4}=7.\n$$\n\nSo, the $n$th term of the sequence can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & =4\\cdot {7}^{n-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want the $n$th term to be greater than $2\\,000\\,000.$\nHence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & >2\\,000\\,000 \\\\ 4\\cdot {7}^{n-1} & >2\\,000\\,000 \\\\ {7}^{n-1} & >500\\,000 \\\\ \\operatorname{log}⁡({7}^{n-1}) & >\\operatorname{log}⁡(500\\,000) \\\\ (n-1)\\operatorname{log}⁡(7) & >\\operatorname{log}⁡(500\\,000) \\\\ n-1 & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(500\\,000)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(7)} \\\\ n & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(500\\,000)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(7)}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEstimating the right-hand side using a calculator, we get:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(500\\,000)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(7)}+1≈7.744\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $8$th term is the first term of the sequence that exceeds $2\\,000\\,000.$"}]}
